{"A":"Refine the measured radix-sort approach (0.011393 s, best observed): retain exact count/prefix/scatter bucket ranges, then test stable byte-radix passes over each bucket’s suffix. Preserve `strcmp` ordering for every valid token, including shorter tokens by correctly initializing and terminating slots; benchmark against the measured result rather than assuming radix is universally faster.","B":"Investigate the counted, prefix-packed buckets plus `qsort` and independent OpenMP bucket sorts (0.011592 s observed). On the ARM64 four-core target, measure scheduling and parallel-startup cutoffs using actual bucket populations, while keeping histogram/scatter race-free and the comparator’s ordering equivalent to `strcmp`. Retain only measured, correct improvements.","C":"Take an independent end-to-end path focused on input/output overhead, which the sorting-focused variants have not changed: investigate buffered token input and batched output while preserving the accepted whitespace/token behavior and exact sorted output. Use safe compact bucket ranges and a nonquadratic sort as support; compare whole-program timings and correctness, not I/O microbenchmarks alone.","G":"Use the measured radix candidate (0.011393 s) as the current incumbent; the parallel-`qsort` candidate is close (0.011592 s), while the other observed `qsort` and heapsort variants were slower (0.012494 s, 0.013690 s, 0.014248 s). Integrate selectively, then validate and measure each complete program with the run’s harness. Do not assume combining individually successful transformations is additive or faster; keep the best integrated candidate that remains correct.","analysis":"The baseline is correct on the reported four cases and measured 0.424067 s. Its insertion sort can require quadratic work within a bucket, and its fixed equal-sized bucket slices are unsafe under skew. All observed candidate families instead count populations and prefix-pack disjoint bucket ranges; the radix and parallel-`qsort` measurements are the strongest results in this run. The target is ARM64 with four CPU cores and no GPU. These are run-specific means, not guarantees across input distributions or after integration; require correctness for every valid input and compare complete-program measurements."}